[!] BLOCKING — PyAV (`av==13.1.0`) is still in pyproject.toml and in the
    three lock files of this commit. PyAV's own code is BSD-3-Clause, but
    its wheels embed an FFmpeg configured with `--enable-gpl` plus x264 /
    x265: GPL-3.0. BUILD_SPEC D9 removes it in favour of our minimal LGPL
    FFmpeg. NO distributed build may contain PyAV. Its entry stays in
    Part 2 only so that every lock pin is accounted for; delete it when
    the locks no longer contain `av`.

[!] TO CHECK IN THE BUILT BUNDLE — Qt modules that are GPL-3.0-only in
    the open-source edition (Qt Virtual Keyboard, Qt Charts, Qt Data
    Visualization, Qt Graphs, Qt Quick 3D, ...) are present in the
    PySide6-Addons wheel. StemForge does not use them and the spec file
    excludes their Python modules, but PyInstaller's QtGui hook collects
    the `platforminputcontexts` plugins, and on PySide6 6.7.3 that folder
    contains `qtvirtualkeyboardplugin` (which links Qt6VirtualKeyboard,
    GPL-3.0). The built bundle must be listed and must contain no GPL-only
    Qt library; otherwise the plugin must be excluded in the build.

[!] PROPRIETARY BUT REDISTRIBUTABLE — the Windows torch wheels embed the
    Intel OpenMP runtime (libiomp5md.dll; "Copyright (C) 1997-2025, Intel
    Corporation"). It is not open source: PyPI lists `intel-openmp` under
    the "Intel End User License Agreement for Developer Tools", which
    allows redistributing the runtime. It does not affect the MIT license
    of StemForge, but its license text must ship with the Windows build.

[!] setuptools (mac-arm64, win-x64 locks: a runtime dependency of torch
    2.10 on Python 3.12) vendors `autocommand` 2.2.2, which is LGPL-3.0.
    StemForge never imports setuptools: excluding it from the bundle is
    the simplest fix; if it stays, the LGPL notice below applies to it.

